Sure! Rust unique. Control memory allocation, deallocation. Yes! Memory safety too. It use ownership, borrowing. Compiler enforce at compile time.

In Rust, each value have single owner. When owner go out of scope, value drop, memory deallocate. This stop common memory issues, like use-after-free, double-free errors.

But, to use values in different parts of program, Rust allow borrowing. Borrowing give temporary access to value, no ownership taken. To prevent invalid, unsafe references, Rust use lifetimes. Lifetimes track value lifetime, ensure references always valid.

